Electrical discharge machining studies that focus on graphite electrodes report consistent relationships between electrode microstructure, process parameters, and machining outcomes for Hastelloy. Nowicki et al. (2022) found that graphite with larger grains increased process efficiency (higher MRR) but returned greater relative tool wear, while finer-grained graphite produced the lowest surface roughness; discharge current and pulse duration were the principal drivers of MRR and Ra, and the time interval most influenced relative electrode wear in their experiments on Hastelloy C-22. {{cite:S1#c1}} The later work by Nowicki et al. (2024) reinforces these conclusions and extends them for negative polarity EDM: fine-grained POCO AF-5 ($1\ \mu$m$)$ improves efficiency and reduces tool wear compared with coarser POCO EDM-180 ($10\ \mu$m$)$, and detached graphite grains alter discharge stability and thus process effectiveness. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Across studies of nickel-based and corrosion-resistant superalloys, peak current and pulse-on time repeatedly emerge as dominant electrical factors controlling both MRR and surface quality. Nas & Kara (2022) reported peak current as the most influential parameter on MRR and Ra—accounting for the largest share of variance in their Taguchi–ANOVA analysis—and identified cryogenic treatment, pulse-on time and peak current as the triad most affecting machinability in their factorial tests. {{cite:S2#c1}} Complementary experimental work on other nickel alloys shows current and pulse duration strongly affect electrode geometry and wear patterns: Ramabalan et al. (2024) observed that those two factors largely determine electrode taper, edge rounding and wear length when producing square holes in Hastelloy B2. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Broader reviews of EDM performance and enhancement techniques place these parameter effects in context and point to multiple routes for reducing TWR and improving MRR. Shastri et al. (2022) summarize that EDM’s main limitations—high TWR, low MRR and elevated roughness—have been addressed experimentally by combinations such as cryogenic treatment, powder-mixed dielectrics and ultrasonic assistance, each altering the measured MRR, TWR and surface integrity in different ways. {{cite:S6#c1}} Work on dielectric modifications and electrode treatments likewise shows practical gains: Sana et al. (2024) report that nanopowder in the dielectric and cryogenic treatment of electrodes produced large reductions in electrode wear and dimensional overcut for brass electrodes in their experiments. {{cite:S7#c1}} General method surveys highlight the potential to tailor coatings or EDC-like approaches to influence surface layers formed during EDM, which may be relevant when selecting electrode materials. {{cite:S5#c1}}

Despite this body of evidence, direct comparative data that evaluate copper versus graphite versus composite electrodes specifically on Hastelloy (MRR, TWR, and parameter sensitivities) are not present in the cited sources. [[NEEDS SOURCE: comparative studies of copper and composite electrodes on Hastelloy]]

rows.bRaw:
Graphite with a larger grain size produced higher material removal rates but also greater relative tool wear when machining Hastelloy C-22, while finer-grained graphite yielded lower surface roughness; these outcomes derive from experiments comparing POCO AF-5 (1 µm) and S-180 (10 µm) electrodes. {{cite:S1#c1}} The same contrast is reported in a later evaluation using the same POCO materials under negative polarity, which attributes improved process efficiency and reduced tool wear to fine-grained graphite and notes that detached graphite grains disturb discharge stability and machining efficiency. {{cite:S3#c1}} Both studies modelled the relationships between machining parameters and outcomes, indicating that discharge current and pulse duration principally govern MRR and Ra for the graphite electrodes tested. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}

Peak current dominated effects on MRR and average surface roughness in a Taguchi-designed factorial study of a corrosion-resistant superalloy subjected to different cryogenic treatments, accounting for 86.43% of MRR variance and 74.79% of Ra variance in ANOVA results. {{cite:S2#c1}} Experimental investigations of Hastelloy B2 report that current and pulse duration substantially affect electrode wear, with unstable sparks causing electrode ends to transform from sharp to hemispherical shapes as heat diffuses and edges erode. {{cite:S4#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}} Regression and ANOVA analyses from titanium EDM work likewise identify amperage (current) as the single most influential factor for MRR and TWR, with percentages near 99% for several response variables. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Methods intended to reduce tool wear or alter surface integrity during EDM include cryogenic treatment, powder-mixed dielectrics, ultrasonic assistance, and electrical discharge coating; reviews synthesize evidence that these associations change MRR, TWR, surface roughness and recast layer characteristics. {{cite:S6#c1}}{{cite:S5#c1}} For example, adding nanopowder to the dielectric and applying cryogenic treatment to electrodes produced substantial reductions in electrode wear and overcut for brass electrodes in experimental studies, with reported improvements up to around 78% for EWR and 81% for dimensional accuracy depending on conditions. {{cite:S7#c1}} These interventions show that electrode material condition and dielectric composition interact with peak current, pulse-on time and spark voltage to modify wear and removal outcomes. {{cite:S7#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}}

[[NEEDS SOURCE: comparative data on composite electrodes for Hastelloy]]

Taken together, the cited literature establishes that electrode material microstructure (graphite grain size), electrical parameters (particularly current and pulse duration), and auxiliary treatments (cryogenic processing, powder-mixed dielectric, coatings) each alter MRR, TWR and surface quality in EDM of nickel-based alloys, while direct experimental comparisons involving composite electrodes on Hastelloy are not provided in the assembled sources. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}}

