The additive manufacturing route imposed by selective laser melting (SLM) produces a characteristic fine martensitic microstructure and very small grain/subgrain dimensions in 18Ni-300 maraging steel, reflecting the extremely high cooling rates during layer-wise melting and solidification. Tan et al. observed an average submicron grain size of $0.31\ \mu$m in SLM-fabricated grade 300 maraging steel and reported a martensitic matrix containing dense, needle-like Ni$_3$X precipitates (X = Ti, Al, Mo) that develop during the process and subsequent thermal treatments; these features were linked to age hardening and to residual-stress states generated by rapid solidification. {{cite:S3#c1}} Ahmadkhaniha et al. likewise found a fine-grained martensitic structure in the as-printed condition and reported similar microstructural appearance across different section planes (xy, xz, yz), with no pronounced crystallographic texture or anisotropy in the as-built material. {{cite:S8#c1}} The SLM process can therefore be regarded as producing near-full-density components with an intrinsically refined martensitic microstructure. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Thermal post-processing substantially modifies these as-built states. Systematic heat treatments relieve residual stress, promote precipitation, and change mechanical anisotropy, while specific intercritical treatments can induce phase reversion. Tan et al. showed that designed heat treatments both increase strengthening by precipitate evolution and reduce residual stress and mechanical anisotropy compared with as-fabricated specimens. {{cite:S3#c1}} Godec et al. examined the interaction of solution treatment, aging and subsequent plasma nitriding, finding that prior heat treatments alter the morphology and chemistry of surface compound layers and affect properties after thermo-chemical treatments. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Austenite reversion in maraging steels processed by SLM is reported to occur when specimens are tempered near the austenite formation range, producing reversed austenite that nucleates preferentially at martensite lath boundaries where Ni enrichment occurs. {{cite:S4#c1}} Khan et al. tempered selective-laser-melted maraging steel in the intercritical $\alpha' + \gamma$ range (about $720\ ^\circ$C in their study) and observed reversed austenite along lath boundaries; they attributed its nucleation to local Ni enrichment following dissolution of intermetallic particles. {{cite:S4#c1}} That study further links the presence of reversed austenite to improved pitting resistance in chloride solution, reporting higher pitting potentials and lower corrosion current densities for the 720°C-tempered specimens compared with as-printed, aged, and homogenized counterparts. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Corrosion behaviour in chloride environments has been assessed in several works, with corrosion performance influenced by microstructure, precipitates, surface condition and post-treatments. Khan et al. evaluated pitting in 3.5 wt% NaCl and found that reversed austenite raised the pitting potential and reduced corrosion rate compared with other heat-treatment states. {{cite:S4#c1}} Ahmadkhaniha et al. compared corrosion of SLM and wrought maraging steels and reported that, despite finer as-printed microstructure, corrosion behaviour after heat treatment and aging was similar between SLM and conventional wrought material; they also identified precipitation as a primary factor controlling both hardness and corrosion response, and showed that electropolishing markedly reduces surface roughness. {{cite:S8#c1}} Godec et al. linked prior heat treatments to improved corrosion resistance after plasma nitriding and noted that omitting appropriate prior aging can lead to defects (cracks, pores) in compound nitride layers that compromise performance. {{cite:S2#c1}}

rows.bRaw:
S3#c1 reports a submicron grain size of $0.31\ \mu\text{m}$ in SLM-produced grade 300 maraging steel, which the authors attribute to cooling rates on the order of $10^{7}\ \text{K/s}$ during processing. {{cite:S3#c1}} This extreme refinement coincides with a martensitic matrix containing dense, needle-shaped Ni3X precipitates that develop in the as-fabricated state and that contribute to age hardening; electron microscopy in that study resolves the precipitate–matrix interfaces and supports an Orowan/coherency strain interpretation of strengthening. {{cite:S3#c1}} Haghdadi et al. 2020 summarise that such unique hierarchical (sub)grains and fine precipitates are characteristic of AM steels and that these features can increase strength, wear resistance and corrosion resistance relative to conventional counterparts. {{cite:S6#c1}}

S8#c1 finds that the as-printed SLM maraging steel exhibits a fine-grained martensitic structure with a surface roughness $R_a\approx 10\ \mu\text{m}$ and no marked texture or anisotropy across the XY, XZ and YZ sections. {{cite:S8#c1}} In that work, aging produces the highest nano-hardness and shifts the corrosion potential to lower values, and the authors conclude that precipitation governs both hardness and corrosion behaviour; electropolishing reduced $R_a$ by about 65%. {{cite:S8#c1}} Afkhami et al. note that processing parameters including build orientation and heat treatment affect mechanical performance, implying similar process sensitivity for microstructure and derived properties in SLM steels. {{cite:S5#c1}}

S4#c1 reports that tempering within the intercritical $\alpha'+\gamma$ range at $720\ ^\circ\text{C}$ produces reversed austenite that nucleates mainly along martensite lath boundaries; local Ni enrichment from dissolved intermetallics promotes this reversion. {{cite:S4#c1}} The same study directly links the presence of this reversed austenite to improved pitting resistance in 3.5 wt% NaCl: the $720\ ^\circ\text{C}$-tempered specimen exhibits a higher pitting potential, a lower corrosion current density, and a lower corrosion rate than as-printed, aged, or homogenized specimens lacking reversed austenite. {{cite:S4#c1}} Khan et al. therefore conclude that austenite reversion can enhance pitting resistance in SLM maraging steel under chloride exposure. {{cite:S4#c1}}

S2#c1 shows that when SLM 18Ni-300 is plasma nitrided without prior aging, cracks form in the compound nitride layer, whereas specimens given prior heat treatment before nitriding show improved wear and corrosion resistance and fewer defects in the nitrided layer. {{cite:S2#c1}} Yan et al. compare SLM and cold-spray composites and report that SLM produces higher densification and lower porosity, which in the SLM case correlates with a lower wear rate and more stable friction behaviour—microstructural evolution of reinforcements is cited as the cause of these property differences. {{cite:S7#c1}} Collectively, these studies indicate that SLM’s intrinsic microstructural state and subsequent heat or thermo-chemical treatments strongly control surface layer quality and chloride-environment corrosion performance. {{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S7#c1}}

