Household decisions about rooftop photovoltaic (PV) systems are shaped by interlinked financial, informational and institutional barriers that studies identify as central impediments to adoption. Multiple empirical analyses highlight that the upfront cost and concerns about returns form a primary inhibitor: Parsad et al. found that government subsidies reduce the perceived initial risk of investment and that low prices and maintenance costs function as financial motivators by easing long‑term return anxieties. {{cite:S3#c1}} Tanveer et al. report that perceived cost has a negative effect on consumers' willingness to adopt solar PV, reinforcing the centrality of price as a barrier in household choices. {{cite:S6#c1}} Aarakit et al. also conclude that affordability determines the type of solar product households choose, and they recommend flexible payment mechanisms to enable income‑constrained households to acquire more expensive systems. {{cite:S7#c1}}

Policy instruments that offset upfront cost therefore matter both as direct incentives and as signals that reduce investment risk, but the literature also shows variation in how policy is experienced on the ground. Parsad et al. emphasise the role of PV subsidies as the primary motivator for household investment, placing policy interventions at the core of uptake strategies. {{cite:S3#c1}} Goel situates rooftop PV targets within national ambitions—India’s solar goals and rooftop share are a policy driver for deployment—while also documenting the challenges in translating national targets into local installations. {{cite:S1#c1}} Kappagantu et al. examined a pilot smart‑grid project and reported implementation barriers encountered by consumers in that local programme context, indicating that project‑level design affects how households experience policy measures. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Beyond finance and formal incentives, lack of knowledge and technical concerns reduce adoption likelihood. Parsad et al. identify technical barriers and deficits in knowledge and awareness as significant predictors of investment decisions, with these non‑financial factors carrying weight alongside economic evaluations. {{cite:S3#c1}} Tanveer et al. similarly find that perceptions of self‑efficacy and beliefs about PV benefits increase willingness to adopt, whereas perceived risk decreases it, indicating that households’ subjective assessments of technology risks and capabilities influence uptake. {{cite:S6#c1}} Sovacool et al. broaden this view by showing that equity and justice dimensions—demographic and spatial disparities among others—shape who can access household solar and under what conditions, implying that informational and social barriers intersect with broader distributional concerns. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Country‑ and context‑specific constraints further mediate barriers to adoption. Reviews of renewable energy in India highlight structural constraints that must be resolved to exploit the country's solar potential, suggesting that favourable resource endowments alone do not remove impediments to deployment. {{cite:S2#c1}} Likewise, sectoral reviews point to institutional, market and investment challenges that policymakers and stakeholders must address to scale rooftop PV. {{cite:S5#c1}} These findings collectively indicate that overcoming household hesitation requires a combination of price‑reducing finance, targeted subsidies, flexible payment options, improved technical information and attention to equity and local implementation dynamics. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S7#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}

rows.bRaw:
India's policy target allocates 40 GW of the planned 100 GW solar capacity to grid-connected rooftop PV, framing rooftop deployment as a central element of national renewable goals. {{cite:S1#c1}} This national commitment shapes the policy instruments and incentives studied in the literature and provides the policy context within which household barriers are assessed. {{cite:S1#c1}}

In survey data from Kerala, government subsidies emerge as the primary financial motivator that reduces the initial investment risk for households considering PV adoption. {{cite:S3#c1}} The same Kerala study finds that lower purchase prices and reduced maintenance costs act as additional financial motivators by alleviating long-run return concerns. {{cite:S3#c1}} Using SEM followed by an ANN ranking, the authors also identify technical barriers and gaps in knowledge and awareness as significant predictors of household investment decisions. {{cite:S3#c1}}

A multi-city household questionnaire in Pakistan reports that higher perceived cost and greater perceived risk both reduce willingness to adopt rooftop PV. {{cite:S6#c1}} That study also shows that social norms and perceived self-efficacy increase willingness to adopt, while openness to technology did not exert a measurable effect in their sample. {{cite:S6#c1}} Together these findings indicate that both economic assessments and psychosocial perceptions influence household choices in ways that complement the Kerala results. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S6#c1}}

Evidence from Uganda indicates affordability and payment flexibility shape not only whether households adopt solar but also which product types they choose. {{cite:S7#c1}} In that nationally representative analysis, flexible payment mechanisms increase uptake of solar home systems and kits, while access to grid electricity is negatively associated with adoption of those off-grid products. {{cite:S7#c1}} The Uganda results therefore link financing modalities and existing service availability to heterogeneous adoption outcomes across rural and urban households. {{cite:S7#c1}}

A pilot smart-grid project survey in Puducherry identifies concrete implementation issues at the consumer level, including available roof space and users’ practical experiences with rooftop systems, as constraints discussed alongside government initiatives. {{cite:S8#c1}} Reviews of India-wide renewable deployment underline a broader set of obstacles to scaling renewables that policy and industry must address. {{cite:S5#c1}} Complementing these India-focused studies, a mixed-methods review of household solar highlights multiple inequities—demographic, spatial, interspecies and temporal—that can be associated with rapid solar expansion and that interact with the barriers above. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Several studies therefore converge on a core pattern: upfront cost, inadequate financing options, and subsidy design materially affect adoption, while technical, informational, and equity-related barriers modulate uptake across places and populations. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S7#c1}} [[NEEDS SOURCE: Quantified effect sizes of subsidies on adoption rates]]

